{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512761-sects-and-global-vampiric-politics","id":1512761,"title":"Sects and Global Vampiric Politics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-25T12:12:56-07:00","id":1512761,"title":"Sects and Global Vampiric Politics","content":"I beca\u200bme curious, wha\u200bt kind of sects a\u200bnd global politics you tend to use in your chronicles?<br /> Personlally:<br /> <br /> <b>Camarilla</b> (mainly Europe and America)<br /> <b>Sabbat</b> (America... mostly)<br /> <b>Anarch</b> <br /> <b>Ashirra</b> (Middle-East)<br /> <b>The Laibon Legacies of the Ebony Kingdom</b> (Africa)<br /> <b>The Einherjar of Skandinavia </b>(&quot;North&quot;)<br /> <b>The Raktasadhu of India</b> (the mutual alliance of the the Daitya, Danava, Nagaraja, Eastern Ravnos, and Wu Zao against the ever-posing threat of the Kuei-jin (mostly the Infinite Thunders Court of southern India and the Golden Court of eastern India.), the Camarilla, the Ashirra, the local Sabbat and the Tzimisce Methuselah Kartarirya and her Tzimsice)<br /> <br /> The <b>Tal'Mahe'Ra </b>and the <b>Inconnu</b> as honorary mention as trouble makers","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T14:16:37-07:00","id":1512771,"title":"","content":"In my chronicles the <b>Camarilla</b> is what it was originally portrayed - a global secret conspiracy governing most vampires (regardless whether their clan has a seat on the Inner Circle). It isn't reduced to a European organization according to recent multi-cultural beliefs. So it includes vampires in the Middle East, India, and Africa as well. I've also toyed with the idea of simply tossing out the whole &quot;Kindred of the East&quot; and adding East Asia to it as well simply because I am not a big fan of the original descriptions of the Gaki or Bushi, and I really don't like the KotE game line. (I have given some thought now and again at what I could replace the KotE with, but so far has not come up with something satisfactory - it has to be more than just &quot;Asian vampires&quot;.) (Note - one problem for the Tremere (and the Giovanni though not the Cappadocians) is that they are in fact far more limited as a European clan than the other clans in the Camarilla. Given their very specific origins and history, they just lack the span and power in many other areas of the world.)<br /> <br /> Terms like Laibon, Ashirra, etc. are just local culture equivalents of the term Kindred, and are mainly just flavor text. They don't represent actual sects.<br /> <br /> There is a small group of devout Muslim vampires who are concerned about protecting their holy cities from others of their kind, but they remain part of the Camarilla. Likewise there are small equivalent groups of other vampires of other religions who take their faith seriously while still being undead. Still more or less just part of the Camarilla or the greater &quot;Camarilla system&quot; when you include the agreements the Camarilla has made with the Independent Clans.<br /> <br /> The <b>Sabbat</b> simply exists wherever it can. It's stronghold is in North America, but it controls parts of Europe, the Middle East, South America, etc. The Regent, who dwells in North America, isn't able to actually control or supervise the Sabbat in these other regions, at best the Regent can send messages and offer advice.<br /> <br /> There are a variety of &quot;independent&quot; city domains around the world who don't recognize the authority of the Camarilla. They all have Princes old and powerful enough they can fight off Justicars and Archons if needed. But in practice, they don't run their domains fundamentally different than the Princes of the Camarilla. They just don't worry about what Justicars, Archons, or Conclaves think of them.<br /> <br /> <b>Anarchs</b> aren't really their own sect. Some Anarchs do want to create their own sect, but the problem is that they would offend all the other Anarchs that have other political ideas. They're an opposition group within the same general population of vampires that the Camarilla's Inner Circle wants to control. Most Anarchs are just pissed off about the local Prince. Only the real revolutionaries want to overthrow the Inner Circle. But since they are a movement and control certain city domains, I'll count them as their own thing here.<br /> <br /> The <b>Inconnu</b> are just a group of very ancient and knowledgeable vampires who are looking for signs of Gehenna, explore the nature of the Kindred existence, understand the intrigues of the Jyhad, and other existential questions. That's why they monitor places, but don't really act. They are mainly just elders who have decided that they really don't know what is going on. They've realized their own ignorance as they notice strange, inexplicable things that keep occurring. So they're now sharing notes with likeminded Kindred.<br /> <br /> The <b>Tal'Mahe'Ra</b> are what I call the &quot;string pullers&quot;. They are the sect that manipulates the other sects as they have fingers in both. At this same level I would place some of the Gehenna cults, groups like the Children of Osiris, certain Infernalist/Baali cults, the Nicktuku, and some additional small groups I've created for my own chronicle setting. This would also include certain Methusalehs who operate just by themselves and whatever pawns they control (like Dark Selina). But the Tal'Mahe'Ra are the largest and most successful. These groups operate at some of the highest levels of the Jyhad - it is pretty much just the surviving true Antedeluvians who are above them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-25T15:57:47-07:00","id":1512786,"title":"","content":"I treat vampire groups like criminal syndicates, they don't really fit into boarders or geographic areas.<br /> <br /> It might be tempting to use vampires like street gangs or low level criminal groups, but the insane amount of wealth and influence individual vampires have, let alone whole groups/clans/factions have access to means that there is no such thing as &quot;localized&quot; influence. Money &amp; power begets more money &amp; power, which means you might live in one place, but you have corporations you control all over the planet.<br /> <br /> Sure there are certain bloodlines which are isolated due to small numbers and anti-social tendencies, but chances are that wherever they hold power, the closest city or town will be influenced by the other clans which accumulate wealth and power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-26T13:00:01-07:00","id":1512904,"title":"","content":"Australia: Camarilla, Sabbat, Anarchs<br /> North America: Camarilla, Sabbat, Anarchs<br /> South America: Camarilla, Sabbat, Anarchs<br /> <br /> Europe: Camarilla, Autarks<br /> Middle East: Camarilla, Autarks, <b>Assamites.</b><br /> South Asia: Camarilla, Autarks<br /> South East Asia: Camarilla, Autarks<br /> East Asia: Camarilla, Autarks<br /> Africa: Camarilla, Autarks,<br /> Antarctica: Caine himself is gorging on the penguins.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>What do I mean by an Autark domain? </b><br /> Well, you've got a prince or elder council that looked at the Camarilla's &quot;Humanity is the path we should all take&quot; policy and said &quot;yeah, nah, fuck that, I like Roads, or I'm still bitterly against Tremere/Giovanni/not using vicissitude which quite frankly freaks the Camarilla out, so we're using dark ages rules for the 21st century, kids.&quot; Unlike the Anarchs, they're quite entrenched and usually make for good neighbours, so as long as they don't do anything too egregious the Cam is happy to let them be. <br /> <br /> <b>The Cam</b> is the global set. Despite European origins It has members from everywhere. The inner circle are diverse.<br /> <b>Anarchs</b> is a catch-all term for 'people who are against the prince' and their people range from reformers to rebels to cults of psychos that need to be put down. Most elders see &quot;free states&quot; as an ugly, transient existence that will right itself as vampires flood to fill the power gaps. <br /> <br /> The <b>Assamites </b>are a serious military sect much like the Sabbat, they're feared by everyone and breakaway Assamites face severe prejudice. They only hold cities in<b> Iran</b> and gained a lot of ground in the time of the Shah and post revolution, but they operate globally and have cells and even small breakaway cults in other regions<br /> The <b>Sabbat </b>has cells and occasionally hold small towns outside of the new world/Australia but only really hold cities in the New World/Australia<br /> <br /> The <b>Ashirra</b> is a bygone. They were built primarily to contain the Assamite threat, which wasn't needed after the Tremere cursed them, and the Baali, <i>who are definitely all dead don't question it too much</i>. They're mostly Cam now.<br /> <b>Followers of Set </b>and <b>Death Clans</b> happily work in any city that welcomes them. Their position in Cam cities is a little tenuous due to their activities having a degree of masquerade risk, fortunately they're good at making friends<br /> <br /> <b>Cathayans</b> still exist but they're usually a peripheral thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 05-26-2023, 04:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-28T10:00:39-07:00","id":1513085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1512761-sects-and-global-vampiric-politics?p=1512786#post1512786\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I treat vampire groups like criminal syndicates, they don't really fit into boarders or geographic areas.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Merchant Guild or Theocracy are another good enough standard scheme that definitely works for creating theories and ideas about how groups, teams, and socities of Vampires ( and Ghouls, and Revenants ) work and function. Certainly there are already some towns and cities in World of Darkness where this comparison is distinctly relevant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T04:31:47-07:00","id":1513188,"title":"","content":"My <b>Camarilla</b> is generally too big and decentralized to really bother with most domains. Princes want as much power as they can get and don't want someone over them telling them what to do, or they'll rebel en masse. Most cities won't see an archon in a hundred years, nevermind justicars. On the otherhand, A small number of cities will get a lot of Camarilla attention, and most of that is usually something along the lines of an archon or Justiciar having personal interests in such a city and doesn't mind throwing their weight around a bit. While neonates might be in awe of such titles and balk at such interference, Most kindred see that for what it is: personal matters. <br /> <br /> Larger Cults often work across multiple cities. Since Gehenna cults are illegal and other kinds of cults often have a masquerade risk, there's a tendency to want to spread the eggs out a bit in case there's a crackdown. Cults are a wonderful tool for storytellers because <s>I can just make shit up as I go along</s> deliver my players unknown and exciting content. Do we have rogue assamites worshipping Kali and looking for kindred to devour? Infernalists seducing the local council or cooking up some nurgle plague? Abyss mystics trying to summon Zgrzlhuzdwagzh'pav? Ravnos kidnapping children to devour fae souls? Metamorphosists? Bahari? In all likelyhood it's just your standard elder Noddists or a New Religious Movement run by Caitiff. Most cults will be subtle and more suited for political games, but there's no shortage of crazy options if you want an extravaganza. Some towns and cities will be entirely composed of cult members, they may call themselves a sect... <br /> <br /> <b>Sabbat</b> are just the biggest cult. While they're famous for their marauding bands of psychopaths, In the few cities where they have uncontested power, they're actually rather chill. Often, they may hold only parts of a city they share with Camarilla or another sect; They may have some truce, or they may be unknown to the wider population.<br /> <br /> The <b>Inconnu </b>are legendary cult shrouded in mystery. Apart from some vague idea of Golconda, itself an unknown concept, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qAXzzHM8zLw\" target=\"_blank\">No one knows who they were or what they were doing</a>, except for the storyteller of course. There's quite a few pretenders stirring the pot who claim to know. <br /> <br /> The <b>TBH</b> is 80% paranoid delusion and excuse for Templars to kick doors in with the remaining 20% being both cults being mistaken for TBH and cults adopting the myth of the TBH. Personally, I find individual facets of the TBH are awesome, but put together it's just too much. <br /> <br /> The <b>Followers of Set </b>are Gnostics and they don't really care how people bend the story to suit them, so long as the gnostic elements remain (Reality is a prison, gods, angels and demons are prison guards) While some take it literally, The Egyptian myth is more a common frame of reference. Settites fully understand that vampires are liars, things get lost in translation, and old material often needs to adapt to new audiences, so in their emotional maturity they're happy to accept radical variations on their mythos. Having cults of mortals and showing off &quot;magic tricks&quot; really grinds on the Camarilla, so cult members secure themselves by making friends, being helpful, and tying themselves up in prestation. <br /> <br /> In contrast, <b>Giovanni </b>and it's families secure themselves by making themselves lapdogs of whoever has the biggest stick. &quot;<i>My entire family serves the prince, are you going against the prince</i>?&quot;. Once the master starts to depend on the Giovanni, they become the slave.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-29T10:11:33-07:00","id":1513205,"title":"","content":"North America: Camarilla and Anarchs with a few independent places like Vancouver that don't hold any real loyalty to any the main groups.<br /> Europe: Camarilla with some Sabbat holdouts that are losing ground to the Camarilla and Anarchs.<br /> England: Mostly independent as the power of Mithras is still potent, but a few Anarchs domains are around.<br /> Asia: Mostly Camarilla or it's local version of it with the Sabbat and Anarchs in a weak state.<br /> Middle East: Ashira and the Camarilla working closely to keep the Sabbat holdovers in check.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}